BLOCK|h1|1|Chapter 32: Zero-Training AI Model Weight Surgery (SafeTensors & GGUF)
BLOCK|p||Traditional machine learning workflows require immense computational power, hundreds of gigabytes of VRAM, and days of GPU cluster training just to align or specialize small AI models.
BLOCK|p||Cluster-lang introduces a revolutionary paradigm: **Zero-Training AI Model Weight Surgery**. By directly decompiling model files, developers can analyze parameter manifolds, compare small student models against large teacher models, and transplant or blend weights directly on disk with **zero heavy training and minimal RAM**.
BLOCK|p||This chapter covers:
BLOCK|p||1. **Model Format Decompilation**: SafeTensors (`.safetensors`) and GGUF (`.gguf`).
BLOCK|p||2. **Data Types & Quantization Formats** (`F32`, `F16`, `BF16`, `Q4_0`, `Q8_0`).
BLOCK|p||3. **Weight Layer Similarity Analysis** via Cosine Distance.
BLOCK|p||4. **Zero-Training Weight Surgery & Manifold Blending**.
BLOCK|p||5. **The `std::ai::model` Standard Library Module**.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|1. Supported Model File Formats
BLOCK|p||Cluster-lang provides native, zero-dependency parsing for the two dominant open-source model standards:
BLOCK|list||**SafeTensors (`.safetensors`)**: The Hugging Face safe zero-copy tensor storage format.
BLOCK|list||**GGUF (`.gguf`)**: The high-performance binary format optimized for llama.cpp, Ollama, and local CPU/GPU inference.
BLOCK|code|python|import std::ai

fn main():
    fmt_st := MODEL_FORMAT_SAFETENSORS
    fmt_gguf := MODEL_FORMAT_GGUF

    put "Format 1: " + model_format_name(fmt_st)    # SafeTensors (HuggingFace)
    put "Format 2: " + model_format_name(fmt_gguf)  # GGUF (llama.cpp)
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|2. Weight Precision & Quantization Formats
BLOCK|p||Cluster-lang inspects parameter precisions down to the bit level:
BLOCK|p||| Format Code | Precision | Description |
BLOCK|p|||:---|:---|:---|
BLOCK|p||| `DTYPE_F32` | 32-bit Float | Standard single-precision IEEE 754 weights |
BLOCK|p||| `DTYPE_F16` | 16-bit Float | Half-precision GPU tensor cores format |
BLOCK|p||| `DTYPE_BF16` | 16-bit Brain Float | Google Brain dynamic range format |
BLOCK|p||| `DTYPE_Q4_0` | 4-bit Quantized | High-efficiency 4-bit block-quantized weights |
BLOCK|p||| `DTYPE_Q8_0` | 8-bit Quantized | Balanced 8-bit quantized weights |
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|3. Parameter Manifold Comparison
BLOCK|p||Before modifying a smaller model, developers can measure semantic alignment and layer drift against a larger, more capable model using vector cosine similarity:
BLOCK|code|python|fn compare_model_layers(student_layer: vector[float], teacher_layer: vector[float]):
    similarity := cosine_similarity(student_layer, teacher_layer)
    put "Layer Cosine Similarity: " + to_text(similarity)

    if similarity < 0.85:
        put "Layer has drifted significantly — target for surgery!"
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|4. Zero-Training Weight Surgery & Linear Blending
BLOCK|p||Rather than spending thousands of dollars training a model from scratch, you can perform direct **weight blending** between a teacher model and a student model:
BLOCK|p||$$\theta_{\text{surged}} = (1 - \alpha) \cdot \theta_{\text{student}} + \alpha \cdot \theta_{\text{teacher}}$$
BLOCK|code|python|fn perform_surgery():
    student_weights: vector[float] = [1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0]
    teacher_weights: vector[float] = [3.0, 4.0, 5.0, 6.0]

    # Blend 50% teacher capability into the student model
    alpha := 0.5
    updated_weights := weight_blend_linear(student_weights, teacher_weights, alpha)

    put "Updated student weights after surgery:"
    i := 0
    while i < list_size(updated_weights):
        put to_text(updated_weights[i])    # 2.0, 3.0, 4.0, 5.0
        i = i + 1
BLOCK|p||By streaming updates directly with `file_seek` and `file_write_byte` (from Chapter 27), entire multi-gigabyte models can be updated in-place with **less than 50MB of RAM**.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|5. Conclusion: All-in-One Systems & AI Language
BLOCK|p||With native multi-dimensional tensors, SIMD matrix multiplication (`@`), zero-glue C interoperability, bare-metal pointer access, and native model weight surgery, Cluster-lang brings together:
BLOCK|list||The **developer speed & readability** of Python.
BLOCK|list||The **raw execution speed & bare-metal power** of C and C++.
BLOCK|list||The **thread and memory safety** of Rust.
BLOCK|list||The **concurrency and clean simplicity** of Go.
BLOCK|list||The **AI native acceleration** of dedicated ML toolchains.
BLOCK|hr||
